hings yes but that  accountability starts rarely at the top  we're very slow and weeding out those  officers who are abusive the Minnesota  case is a very important case all the  officers who were involved were also  indicted we said you are your brother's  keeper not only do you have an  obligation to stop a person that's not a  cop from committing a crime you have an  obligation and responsibility to stop  your brother officer from committing a  crime when you were on the force did you  feel that that was the message no not at  all that was not the message in fact if  you did it you would be targeted and you  would be tocall the rat you will be  called someone that is hurting the blue  a uniform or the blue wall it was just  the opposite do you think that's the  situation or the climate that we're in  now it is it is still there so we must  have mechanism in place where an officer  can clearly number one stop abuse from  happening immediately number two be able  to report it without jeopardizing his  public safety at the same time because  policing is real fighting crime is real  the New York Police Department has taken  criticism for the way it's handled some  of the things one person described it as  overly aggressive with the peaceful  protesters and not aggressive enough  with that criminal element why are we  seeing that potentially policing is the  only profession that we state if you're  in the career you could do every job in  the career and that is not true because  you are know how to fight crime doesn't  mean you know how to deal with a  sensitive issue like handling protests  we have to change how we assign police  officers and be honest in doing so then  you see these officers who are receiving  an onslaught of insult and they are  poise they handle themselves well they  really deescalate situations and then  you look at others who spent a lifetime  of only going through kicking in doors  going after bad guys with guns and in  their mind everyone is a